  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe next few days will feature the passage of a couple\nshortwaves tracking east-southeasterly from the Great Lakes \nthrough the region. The first shortwave will continue to push \nthrough during the overnight hours into this afternoon with \nclouds and occasional rain showers. QPF amounts between 0.10 to \n0.25 inches are expected for the western Mohawk Valley and \nAdirondacks with lesser amounts elsewhere. Breezy conditions \nwill develop once again later this morning into the afternoon \nwith gusts around 20 to 30 mph. Drier weather returns tonight as\nthe first shortwave departs with precipitation chances \nincreasing again on Tuesday as the next shortwave approaches. \nThis one will be weaker and track slightly more northward. This \nwill favor shower chances mainly for areas along and north of \nI-90 with QPF amounts generally less than 0.10 inches. This \nshortwave departs by Tuesday night with zonal flow developing \non Wednesday. There may be enough of a lake to 850 hPa \ntemperature difference on Wednesday (850 hPa temperatures below\n0C) to generate a few showers with west to northwesterly flow \nremaining in place. These look to be focused within the western\nMohawk Valley with upslope showers across the Adirondacks.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nConfidence is high in the development of a large scale trough \nlater this week into next weekend bringing a push of cooler air.\nMultiple shortwaves look to rotate around the main trough \nduring this time. However, the overall timing and track of any \nsurface lows remain very uncertain with continued large \nvariations amongst all of the forecast models. It does appear \nthat a surface low will approach from the Ohio Valley on \nThursday with eventual energy transfer to a coastal low sometime\nThursday night or Friday, forming a Miller-B type Nor'easter. \nHow and when these storms interact and track will determine \nprecipitation chances and type. The latest deterministic GFS \ncontinues to show a system close enough to bring widespread \nprecipitation which would include some wet snow, especially for \nthe higher elevations. The deterministic CMC continues to show a\nstorm track far enough to the south and east to bring little or\nno precipitation at all with the deterministic ECMWF in-between\nthe GFS/CMC with generally light precipitation which would \nlikely be valley rain and high elevation wet snow. Each ensemble\nand AI models continues to show wide ranges in storm track so \nconfidence remains low on which solution is more likely at this \ntime. We will continue to provide updates as details become more\nclear.",
